ange of the speed $S?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981296/state.json" "file_sha256" "7921767fdcfd78d2a876f7d806077e36fb55cc7ceb66275ddc01ee0cdadfe93f" "basis" 885 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-143094" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **speed** $S$ of an object **decreases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **square of the travel time** $t.$*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *speed* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *square of the travel time.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the speed *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{t}^{2}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981296/state.json" "file_sha256" "7921767fdcfd78d2a876f7d806077e36fb55cc7ceb66275ddc01ee0cdadfe93f" "basis" 885 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-143094" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981296/activity-metadata.json" "