veloper/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981296/state.json" "file_sha256" "7921767fdcfd78d2a876f7d806077e36fb55cc7ceb66275ddc01ee0cdadfe93f" "basis" 885 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-143061" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k{V}^{2},\\,k>0"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981296/state.json" "file_sha256" "7921767fdcfd78d2a876f7d806077e36fb55cc7ceb66275ddc01ee0cdadfe93f" "basis" 885 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-143061" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{V}^{2}},\\,k>0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981296/state.json" "file_sha256" "7921767fdcfd78d2a876f7d806077e36fb55cc7ceb66275ddc01ee0cdadfe93f" "basis" 885 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-521" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A helium balloon loses $20$ grams of helium every second. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the mass $M$ of the balloon?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981296/example-521.json" "file_sha256" "82a94df7cd6e5ebc9808a806c2a21ab5425ff93fa9a46d8ecd654edca9f0c49f" "basis" 885 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-521" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If the mass of the balloon is $M$ grams, then the rate of change of the mass is $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}M}{\\textrm{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in seconds.\n\nSince $20$ grams are lost every second, the rate of change of the balloon's mass is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}M}{\\textrm{d}t}=-20.\n$$\n\nNotice that the right-hand side is *negative* because the balloon is *losing* mass." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981296/example-521.json" "file_sha256" "82a94df7cd6e5ebc9808a806c2a21ab5425ff93fa9a46d8ecd654edca9f0c49f" "basis" 885 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-522" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The volume of water $V(t)$ in a leaking tank decreases at a rate that is proportional to the time $t$ (in minutes) since the leak started. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the volume of water at time $t?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981296/example-522.json" "file_sha256" "360fdfc935174ac42325438dbabd8e6e88395ea04267fab276787f744f0043af" "basis" 885 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-522" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of water $V(t)$ in a leaking tank **decreases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to the **time** $t$ (in minutes) since the leak started. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume* of water varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}$ is *proportional to* the *time* since the water begins to leak.  In other words,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} \\propto t.\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\propto$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the volume of water *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} = -kt.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981296/example-522.json" "file_sha256" "360fdfc935174ac42325438dbabd8e6e88395ea04267fab276787f744f0043af" "basis" 885 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-10071" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The area $A$ of a forest decreases with time due to deforestation. The rate of change of the forest area is proportional to the square of the area. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the area at time $t?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981296/example-10071.json" "file_sha256" "a04d64a20c202c25e56ac95f36a0e9f8e8ed06845571e72b43627af8fb8f08fe" "basis" 885 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-10071" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** $A$ of a forest **decreases** with time due to deforestation. The **rate** of change of the forest area is **proportional** to the **area squared**.  *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}$ is *proportional to* the *area squared.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} \\propto A^2.\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\propto$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the area *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = -kA^2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981296/example-10071.json" "file_sha256" "a04d64a20c202c25e56ac95f36a0e9f8e8ed06845571e72b43627af8fb8f08fe" "basis" 885 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-10073" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The volume of water $V$ in a bucket decreases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the square of the volume of water. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the volume of water $V?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981296/example-10073.json" "file_sha256" "28ad930a4b2c8365a62358cfb07500640d3472f4ec035ebfbbd9f31b28443883" "basis" 885 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-10073" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The  **volume of water** $V$  in a bucket **decreases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the ** square of the volume of water** $V.$*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume of water* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *square of the volume.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{V^2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\propto$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the volume of water *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} = -\\dfrac{k}{V^2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981296/example-10073.json" "file_sha256" "28ad930a4b2c8365a62358cfb07500640d3472f4ec035ebfbbd9f31b28443883" "basis" 885 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-143058" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The height $H$ of a tree increases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the tree's height at that moment.\nWhat differential equation describes the rate of change of the height $H?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041088/state.json" "file_sha256" "5fc02c133ee4753e28d2d3b68272f60597186815196147075bc530f44368281f" "basis" 1480 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041088/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-143058" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **height** $H$ of a tree **increases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **tree's height** $H$ at that moment. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *height* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *tree's height.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{H}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the height *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{H}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041088/state.json" "file_sha256" "5fc02c133ee4753e28d2d3b68272f60597186815196147075bc530f44368281f" "basis" 1480 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041088/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-143058" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041088/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b2878443cc0008e933413ef7a22fc8902a26adb7c74953e2c18905a7f30118ab" "basis" 1480 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041088/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-143058" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041088/state.json" "file_sha256" "5fc02c133ee4753e28d2d3b68272f60597186815196147075bc530f44368281f" "basis" 1480 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041088/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-143058" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "v