city is proportional to the square of the velocity. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the velocity at time $t?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d9f525c4d5985d6aa8793a8be364b558d2b13f59fa68f5eb3b50e0d77a796da" "basis" 1255 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-143036" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **velocity** $v$ of a stone falling through some water to the bottom of a lake **decreases** with time. The **rate** of change of the stone's velocity is **proportional** to the **square of the velocity**.  *\n\nWe note the following: \n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *velocity* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *velocity squared.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}∝{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the velocity *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-k{v}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d9f525c4d5985d6aa8793a8be364b558d2b13f59fa68f5eb3b50e0d77a796da" "basis" 1255 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/edb-import/verification.jso