eveloper/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d9f525c4d5985d6aa8793a8be364b558d2b13f59fa68f5eb3b50e0d77a796da" "basis" 1255 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-143094" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{t},\\,k>0"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d9f525c4d5985d6aa8793a8be364b558d2b13f59fa68f5eb3b50e0d77a796da" "basis" 1255 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-143094" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{S}^{2}},\\,k>0"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d9f525c4d5985d6aa8793a8be364b558d2b13f59fa68f5eb3b50e0d77a796da" "basis" 1255 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-143094" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{t}^{2}},\\,k>0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d9f525c4d5985d6aa8793a8be364b558d2b13f59fa68f5eb3b50e0d77a796da" "basis" 1255 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-521" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A helium balloon loses $20$ grams of helium every second. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the mass $M$ of the balloon?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/example-521.json" "file_sha256" "82a94df7cd6e5ebc9808a806c2a21ab5425ff93fa9a46d8ecd654edca9f0c49f" "basis" 1255 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-521" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If the mass of the balloon is $M$ grams, then the rate of change of the mass is $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}M}{\\textrm{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in seconds.\n\nSince $20$ grams are lost every second, the rate of change of the balloon's mass is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}M}{\\textrm{d}t}=-20.\n$$\n\nNotice that the right-hand side is *negative* because the balloon is *losing* mass." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/example-521.json" "file_sha256" "82a94df7cd6e5ebc9808a806c2a21ab5425ff93fa9a46d8ecd654edca9f0c49f" "basis" 1255 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-522" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The volume of water $V(t)$ in a leaking tank decreases at a rate that is proportional to the time $t$ (in minutes) since the leak started. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the volume of water at time $t?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/example-522.json" "file_sha256" "360fdfc935174ac42325438dbabd8e6e88395ea04267fab276787f744f0043af" "basis" 1255 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-522" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of water $V(t)$ in a leaking tank **decreases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to the **time** $t$ (in minutes) since the leak started. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume* of water varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}$ is *proportional to* the *time* since the water begins to leak.  In other words,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} \\propto t.\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\propto$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the volume of water *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} = -kt.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/example-522.json" "file_sha256" "360fdfc935174ac42325438dbabd8e6e88395ea04267fab276787f744f0043af" "basis" 1255 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-10071" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The area $A$ of a forest decreases with time due to deforestation. The rate of change of the forest area is proportional to the square of the area. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the area at time $t?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/example-10071.json" "file_sha256" "a04d64a20c202c25e56ac95f36a0e9f8e8ed06845571e72b43627af8fb8f08fe" "basis" 1255 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-10071" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** $A$ of a forest **decreases** with time due to deforestation. The **rate** of change of the forest area is **proportional** to the **area squared**.  *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}$ is *proportional to* the *area squared.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} \\propto A^2.\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\propto$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the area *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = -kA^2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/example-10071.json" "file_sha256" "a04d64a20c202c25e56ac95f36a0e9f8e8ed06845571e72b43627af8fb8f08fe" "basis" 1255 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-10073" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The volume of water $V$ in a bucket decreases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the square of the volume of water. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the volume of water $V?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/example-10073.json" "file_sha256" "28ad930a4b2c8365a62358cfb07500640d3472f4ec035ebfbbd9f31b28443883" "basis" 1255 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-10073" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The  **volume of water** $V$  in a bucket **decreases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the ** square of the volume of water** $V.$*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume of water* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *square of the volume.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{V^2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\propto$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the volume of water *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} = -\\dfrac{k}{V^2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/example-10073.json" "file_sha256" "28ad930a4b2c8365a62358cfb07500640d3472f4ec035ebfbbd9f31b28443883" "basis" 1255 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037103/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-353452" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A reservoir releases $14$ cubic meters of water every hour. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the volume of water $V(t)$ remaining in the reservoir? Assume that the time $t$ is measured in hours.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=$  \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-353452" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If the volume of water remaining is $V,$ then the rate of change of the volume is $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in hours.\n\nSince $14$ cubic meters of water are released each hour, the rate of change of the volume is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-14.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is negative because the volume of water is *decreasing*." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-353452" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5352fdf86e6abe82c17a6566dd848951ebe0972ce1864ab53dbaae9fcdf8bb84"} {"question" "q-353452" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-353452" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-14"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-51251" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The population of pines in a forest increases by $100$ trees every year. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the population $P(t)$ of the pines? Assume that the time $t$ is measured in years." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-51251" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If the pines population is $P,$ then the rate of change of the population is $\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in years.\n\nSince $100$ new pines are added every year, the rate of change of the population of the pines is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=100.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-51251" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5352fdf86e6abe82c17a6566dd848951ebe0972ce1864ab53dbaae9fcdf8bb84"} {"question" "q-51251" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-51251" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=100"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=100+P"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=100P"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=100k,\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{100}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-51251" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=100"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-38308" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "During the winter months, the energy consumption of an average household increases by $1\\,\\text{kWh}$ every week. What differential equation describes the rate of change of energy consumption $E(t)$ (measured in $\\text{kWh}$) of an average household during these months? Assume that the time $t$ is measured in weeks." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-38308" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If the energy consumption of an average household is $E\\,\\text{kWh},$ then the rate of change of the energy consumption is $\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in weeks.\n\nSince an additional $1\\,\\text{kWh}$ is consumed every week, the rate of change of the energy consumption is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}t}=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-38308" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5352fdf86e6abe82c17a6566dd848951ebe0972ce1864ab53dbaae9fcdf8bb84"} {"question" "q-38308" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-38308" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}t}=-1"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}t}=-k,\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}t}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}t}=e"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}t}=k,\\,k>0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-38308" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}t}=1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-119053" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Lucy reads $6$ pages of her new book each day. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the number of pages $P(t)$ Lucy has left to read? Assume that the time $t$ is measured in days." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-119053" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If the number of pages Lucy has left to read is $P$, then the rate of change of the number of the pages is $\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in days.\n\nSince Lucy reads $6$ pages every day, the rate of change of the number of pages she has left is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=-6.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is negative because the number of pages Lucy has left to read is *decreasing*." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-119053" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5352fdf86e6abe82c17a6566dd848951ebe0972ce1864ab53dbaae9fcdf8bb84"} {"question" "q-119053" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-119053" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=-6"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=-6P"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=6"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=6P"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=P-6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-119053" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=-6"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-353449" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The number of completed online course enrollments increases by $18$ enrollments every hour during a promotional event. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the number of enrollments $E(t)?$ Assume that the time $t$ is measured in hours.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}t}=$  \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-353449" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If the number of enrollments is $E,$ then the rate of change of the number of enrollments is $\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in hours.\n\nSince $18$ new enrollments are added every hour, the rate of change of the number of enrollments is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}t}=18.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-353449" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5352fdf86e6abe82c17a6566dd848951ebe0972ce1864ab53dbaae9fcdf8bb84"} {"question" "q-353449" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-353449" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "18"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-38595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Peter has planted a tree. The height $h$ of the tree increases at a rate that is proportional to the height of the tree. What differential equation describes the rate of growth of the height of the tree at time $t?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-38595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **height** of the tree **increases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to the **height** of the tree.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *height* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *height.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}∝h.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the height *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=kh.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-38595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5352fdf86e6abe82c17a6566dd848951ebe0972ce1864ab53dbaae9fcdf8bb84"} {"question" "q-38595" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-38595" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-kh,\\,\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=k+h,\\,\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=kh,\\,\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=th"] ["math" "t\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=h"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-38595" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=kh,\\,\\,k>0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-38591" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "When heated, the concentration $C(t)$ of oxygen in water decreases at a rate that's proportional to the concentration of oxygen in the water at that moment. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the oxygen concentration at time $t?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-38591" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*When heated, the **concentration** $C(t)$ of oxygen in water **decreases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to the **concentration** of oxygen in the water at that moment.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *concentration* of oxygen in water varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *concentration* of oxygen in the water. In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}∝C.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the concentration of oxygen *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=-kC.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-38591" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5352fdf86e6abe82c17a6566dd848951ebe0972ce1864ab53dbaae9fcdf8bb84"} {"question" "q-38591" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-38591" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=-kC,\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=-tC"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=k-C,\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=kC,\\,k>0"] ["math" "t\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=-C"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-38591" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=-kC,\\,k>0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-51254" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The number of bacteria in a particular crop at any given moment decreases at a rate that is proportional to the number of bacteria at that moment.\nWhat differential equation describes the rate of change of the number of bacteria $B$ at time $t?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-51254" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **number of bacteria** in a particular crop at any given moment **decreases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to **the number of bacteria** at that moment..* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *number of bacteria* in a particular crop varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *number of bacteria* at the moment. In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}∝B.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the number of bacteria *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=-kB.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-51254" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5352fdf86e6abe82c17a6566dd848951ebe0972ce1864ab53dbaae9fcdf8bb84"} {"question" "q-51254" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-51254" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{B},\\,\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=-kB,\\,\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=B"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{B},\\,\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=k-\\frac{1}{B},\\,\\,k>0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-51254" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=-kB,\\,\\,k>0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-38589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Some people in a community contracted an unknown virus. The number of people infected by the virus increases at a rate that is proportional to the number of people infected by the virus. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the number of people who contracted the virus, $V,$ at time $t?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-38589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **number of people** infected by the virus **increases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to the **number of people** infected by the virus.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *number of people* infected by the virus varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *number of people* infected. In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}∝V.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the number of people infected by the virus  *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=kV.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-38589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/mu