ses with time. The rate of change of the stone's velocity is proportional to the square of the velocity. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the velocity at time $t?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-143036" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **velocity** $v$ of a stone falling through some water to the bottom of a lake **decreases** with time. The **rate** of change of the stone's velocity is **proportional** to the **square of the velocity**.  *\n\nWe note the following: \n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *velocity* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *velocity squared.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}∝{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the velocity *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-k{v}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-143036" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/activity-metadata.json" 