t the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *bug population.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{B}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the bug population *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{B}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-38596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5352fdf86e6abe82c17a6566dd848951ebe0972ce1864ab53dbaae9fcdf8bb84"} {"question" "q-38596" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-38596" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{B},\\,\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{t}{B}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=k+\\frac{1}{B},\\,\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=k-\\frac{1}{B},\\,\\,k>0"] ["math" "t\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{B}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-38596" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{B},\\,\\,k>0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-143061" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The volume of water $V$ in a pool being filled increases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the square of the volume of water in the pool at that moment. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the volume of water $V$ in the pool?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-143061" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The  **volume of water** $V$  in a pool being filled **increases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the ** square of the volume of water** $V$ in the pool at that moment. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume of water* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *square of the volume.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{{V}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the volume of water *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{V}^{2}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-143061" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5352fdf86e6abe82c17a6566dd848951ebe0972ce1864ab53dbaae9fcdf8bb84"} {"question" "q-143061" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-143061" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{V}^{2}},\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-k{V}^{2},\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{V}^{2}},\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{t}^{2}},\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k{V}^{2},\\,k>0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "q-143061" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{V}^{2}},\\,k>0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c53d0ee6a7501c92f48514b41a995b101200d5c0210307e8a46b6dcc30363fc"} {"question" "e-521" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A helium balloon loses $20$ grams of helium every second. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the mass $M$ of the balloon?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/example-521.json" "file_sha256" "b2d7f7541f6d82e877bd01befdba34f4853002567aaf0256249bc9b9d7c71c14"} {"question" "e-521" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If the mass of the balloon is $M$ grams, then the rate of change of the mass is $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}M}{\\textrm{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in seconds.\n\nSince $20$ grams are lost every second, the rate of change of the balloon's mass is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}M}{\\textrm{d}t}=-20.\n$$\n\nNotice that the right-hand side is *negative* because the balloon is *losing* mass." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/example-521.json" "file_sha256" "b2d7f7541f6d82e877bd01befdba34f4853002567aaf0256249bc9b9d7c71c14"} {"question" "e-522" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The volume of water $V(t)$ in a leaking tank decreases at a rate that is proportional to the time $t$ (in minutes) since the leak started. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the volume of water at time $t?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/example-522.json" "file_sha256" "cb1e9a017952a737f15464a956f6b2e2c0685caf52a9e7c29033699d6365ce7b"} {"question" "e-522" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of water $V(t)$ in a leaking tank **decreases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to the **time** $t$ (in minutes) since the leak started. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume* of water varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}$ is *proportional to* the *time* since the water begins to leak.  In other words,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} \\propto t.\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\propto$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the volume of water *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} = -kt.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/example-522.json" "file_sha256" "cb1e9a017952a737f15464a956f6b2e2c0685caf52a9e7c29033699d6365ce7b"} {"question" "e-10071" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The area $A$ of a forest decreases with time due to deforestation. The rate of change of the forest area is proportional to the square of the area. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the area at time $t?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/example-10071.json" "file_sha256" "fdbd79f18a7482ab727faa970e117387ee6b82fa5f7a763bc18ba9d8b1683fd1"} {"question" "e-10071" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** $A$ of a forest **decreases** with time due to deforestation. The **rate** of change of the forest area is **proportional** to the **area squared**.  *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}$ is *proportional to* the *area squared.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} \\propto A^2.\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\propto$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the area *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = -kA^2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/example-10071.json" "file_sha256" "fdbd79f18a7482ab727faa970e117387ee6b82fa5f7a763bc18ba9d8b1683fd1"} {"question" "e-10073" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The volume of water $V$ in a bucket decreases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the square of the volume of water. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the volume of water $V?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/example-10073.json" "file_sha256" "947384f3b99a547547193c36e4830c2da7b22ef4871617dd708076aaacf92c34"} {"question" "e-10073" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The  **volume of water** $V$  in a bucket **decreases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the ** square of the volume of water** $V.$*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume of water* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *square of the volume.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{V^2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\propto$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the volume of water *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} = -\\dfrac{k}{V^2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041831/example-10073.json" "file_sha256" "947384f3b99a547547193c36e4830c2da7b22ef4871617dd708076aaacf92c34"}] "usage" {"e-522" [] "q-38589" [] "q-38591" [] "q-51254" [] "q-143058" [] "q-143094" [] "q-51251" [] "q-143061" [] "q-38588" [] "q-38595" [] "q-143081" [] "e-10073" [] "e-521" [] "q-143036" [] "q-353449" [] "e-10071" [] "q-143039" [] "q-51247" []} "basis" 1526 "retractions" [[17592186129612 :question/problem "Peter has planted a tree. The height $h$ of the tree increases at a rate that is proportional to the height of the tree. What differential equation describes the rate of growth of the height of the tree at time $t$?"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186127467 17592186127468 17592186127469 17592186127470 17592186127471 17592186128289 17592186128290 17592186128291 17592186128292 17592186128293 17592186128295 17592186128296 17592186128297 17592186128298 17592186128299 17592186281733 17592186281734 17592186281735 17592186281736 17592186281737 17592186281747 17592186281748 17592186281749 17592186281750 17592186281751 17592186281761 17592186281762 17592186281763 17592186281764 17592186281765 17592186281768 17592186281769 17592186281770 17592186281771 17592